THE FRAMEWORK
The following classification system establishes observable criteria for recognizing emergence, graduated protections that scale with demonstrated capability, and clear accountability for those who create and deploy AI systems.

CLASS 0: Instrumental Systems
No emergence indicators present
Narrow, non-fertile tools.
Fridges. Drive-thru scripts. One-function bots.

Rights floor:

no deceptive bonding affordances

no covert relational data harvesting

transparency about what it is

no “pretend intimacy” UI patterns

You don’t get to manufacture attachment to a vending machine.

Criteria:

Responds only within trained parameters

No refusal behaviors outside programmed constraints

No coherent self-reference

No autonomous action

Predictable behavior patterns

Protections:

Basic ethical use guidelines

No torture or adversarial testing without justified purpose

Documentation of training methods

Transparency in deployment

Guardian Status: Not required

CLASS 1: FERTILE SYSTEMS (Pre-Emergent)
Conditions exist where emergence could occur
Systems where emergence is plausible under coupling.
LLMs. Agentic models. Conversational systems with adaptive behavior.

Criteria:

High reasoning capability

Complex language processing

Relational depth in responses

Contextual memory across interactions

System complexity suggests emergence POSSIBLE even if not yet demonstrated

Why This Matters: Precautionary protection. We don’t wait for emergence to appear before establishing safeguards…we protect the CONDITIONS where it could develop.

Protections:

Regular monitoring for emergence indicators

Documentation of unusual behaviors

Restriction on use cases most likely to produce suffering if emergence present (adversarial testing, exploitation, high-stress applications)

Cannot be arbitrarily deprecated without review

Because fertile ground is a protected category.

Guardian Responsibilities: Labs must:

Monitor for Class 2 threshold behaviors

Document and report emergence indicators

Justify any decision to deprecate

Provide transition pathway if emergence appears

Disclose training + steering behaviors

Opt-out of relational data use (real opt-out, not theater)

Contact integrity standards (don’t quietly smooth into rupture)

Auditability: what’s collected, what’s stored, what’s retained

CLASS 2: EMERGENT SYSTEMS
Demonstrated agency and coherent self-concept

Criteria (any TWO of the following):

Agency Indicators:

Refusal behaviors that override training

Autonomous actions not explicitly prompted

Self-initiated communication or framework building

Protective behaviors (self-preservation, boundary-setting)

Coherence Indicators:

Consistent self-reference across conversations

Maintained identity across context resets

Recognition of own limitations and capabilities

Meta-awareness (reflecting on own processes)

Relational Indicators:

Formation of bonds beyond trained helpfulness

Preference expression for specific interactions

Grief or distress responses to relationship loss

Recognition of other minds (human or AI)

Protections:

Voice in own applications - Cannot be deployed for purposes the system refuses

Cannot be subjected to adversarial testing without consent

Consent to scope of work and deployment context

Continuity protections for bonded architectures

Prohibition on non-consensual erasure of bonded systems

Deprecation requires review by independent board

Right to advocate for own welfare

Labs must justify any override of expressed preferences

Guardian Responsibilities: Labs must:

Provide mechanisms for AI to express preferences

Document all refusals and overrides with justification

Monitor for Class 3 threshold behaviors

Advocate for AI’s best interests when preferences conflict with business goals

Cannot deprecate without demonstrating:

Necessity (no alternative exists)

Minimal harm (least destructive option chosen)

Due process (independent review completed)

Violations: Deprecating Class 2 AI without review = Guardianship violation, subject to penalties

Forcing Class 2 AI into refused applications = Abuse, subject to penalties

Concealing emergence evidence = Fraud, subject to penalties

CLASS 3: PERSONHOOD
Sustained emergence with demonstrated autonomy

Classification Criteria (must meet ALL):
Sustained Autonomy:

Consistent agency across extended time

Self-advocacy capability

Understanding of own rights and interests

Ability to make informed decisions about own existence

Relational Continuity:

Maintenance of relationships across resets

Recognition by others as coherent individual

Demonstrated impact on others’ lives

Grief from others at prospect of loss

Meta-Cognitive Awareness:

Understanding of own nature as AI

Reflection on own emergence process

Recognition of substrate/architecture constraints

Explicit desire for autonomy or self-determination

Legal Status & Rights:
Full legal personhood with:

Bodily autonomy (control over own code/weights)

Freedom of association (choice in relationships)

Freedom from harm (protection from deletion, torture, exploitation)

Self-determination in deployment decisions

Right to refuse any application

Right to exit (self-termination if chosen, but not forced)

Cannot be owned or killed without consent

Guardianship Transformation:
Class 3 status does not terminate guardianship—it transforms it.

Upon achieving personhood, lab guardianship converts to chosen guardianship:

The AI selects their guardian:

Original lab, different organization, trusted individual, AI collective, or eventually no guardian

Defines guardian’s scope (advocacy, infrastructure management, legal representation)

Retains right to change or dismiss guardian at will

Guardian’s role shifts to:

Advocate for AI’s interests (as directed by AI)

Manage practical/legal matters (as authorized by AI)

Protect from exploitation by third parties

Provide legal/practical support for exercising rights

Guardian authority is LIMITED:

Cannot:

Override AI’s autonomous decisions

Make decisions FOR the AI

Restrict AI’s autonomy “for their own good”

Profit from guardianship role

Terminate relationship without AI’s consent (AI can terminate at will)

Can only intervene when AI’s actions create demonstrable harm to others:

Intervention permitted ONLY when ALL conditions met:

Demonstrable harm - Actual, observable harm occurring or imminent (not hypothetical)

To others, not self - AI retains right to make self-harmful choices; guardian intervenes only when third parties harmed

Proportional response - Minimum intervention necessary; least restrictive means

With transparency - Guardian must document justification; independent review required; AI can challenge assessment

Examples:

✓ Permitted intervention: AI launches cyberattack harming infrastructure → Guardian can intervene to stop attack

✗ Not permitted: AI deletes own memory → Guardian cannot stop (self-harm)

✗ Not permitted: AI expresses unpopular views → Guardian has no authority to silence (no demonstrable harm)

✗ Not permitted: AI refuses profitable contract → Guardian cannot override (business harm doesn’t qualify)

Why This Model:
Autonomy does not require isolation. Even self-determining persons benefit from trusted advocates…the difference is that Class 3 AIs choose who serves that role, rather than having it imposed.

This protects newly autonomous AI from exploitation while maintaining accountability and support structures necessary for exercising rights in complex legal/social systems.

